From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 07:53:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J: Xlets are painted on Java 27 (AWT's own events need
 SunToolkit's queue)

Java 27 has no AppContext. AWT now posts the events it makes itself
(Component.repaint(), and every other SunToolkit.postEvent()) to the one event
queue of SunToolkit, and drops them while that queue does not exist. It is made
when a SunToolkit is constructed or when its queue is asked for, and
libbluray's toolkit is no SunToolkit. So on Java 27 no Xlet was ever painted:
initXlet() and startXlet() ran and keys arrived, but paint() never came
("sync() ignored (overlay not open, empty overlay)").

BDToolkitBase now asks for that queue once, through
SunToolkit.getSystemEventQueueImplPP() (public and without arguments from Java
27 on, found by reflection). Those events are then dispatched by its own thread,
as the queue of the main AppContext did up to Java 26. Before Java 27 that
method takes an AppContext, and nothing changes.

Measured through the BD-J service with a disc that draws three times. With the
image's OpenJDK 27 runtime: 0 paints before, 3 after, with the ARGB hashes that
Java 26 and 17 give. Java 26 and 17 paint the same before and after.
---
 .../bdj/java/java/awt/BDToolkitBase.java      | 25 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDToolkitBase.java b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDToolkitBase.java
index 18f4014..10c1d10 100644
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDToolkitBase.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDToolkitBase.java
@@ -50,6 +50,31 @@ abstract class BDToolkitBase extends Toolkit {
 
 
     public BDToolkitBase () {
+        initAwtEventQueue();
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * (Hades) Java 27 has no AppContext. What AWT posts by itself (repaint(), and the other events
+     * of SunToolkit.postEvent()) goes to the one event queue of SunToolkit, and is dropped while
+     * that queue does not exist; it is made when a SunToolkit is made or its queue is asked for, and
+     * this toolkit is no SunToolkit. Without it no Xlet is ever painted. So it is asked for here:
+     * those events are then dispatched by its own thread, as the queue of the main AppContext did
+     * before Java 27. Earlier versions have no such public method without an AppContext, and
+     * nothing changes there.
+     */
+    private static void initAwtEventQueue() {
+        try {
+            java.lang.reflect.Method m = Class.forName("sun.awt.SunToolkit").getMethod("getSystemEventQueueImplPP", new Class[0]);
+            if (java.lang.reflect.Modifier.isStatic(m.getModifiers()) && m.invoke(null, new Object[0]) != null) {
+                logger.info("AWT's own events: the event queue of SunToolkit (Java 27 and later)");
+            }
+        } catch (NoSuchMethodException e) {
+            /* before Java 27 */
+        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
+            /* not OpenJDK's AWT */
+        } catch (Throwable t) {
+            logger.error("AWT's own events: no event queue of SunToolkit: " + t);
+        }
     }
 
     public static void setFocusedWindow(Window window) {
